// Nexa TC Application — Cursor AI rules
// Rules layout follows patterns from https://github.com/PatrickJS/awesome-cursorrules

// Project context
// Describe the application purpose, stack, and architecture here as the codebase grows.
// Stack-specific guidance lives in rules-new/*.mdc; enable or copy the files that match your stack.

// Versioning — v.X.Y.Z (must align with VERSION file and Git tags)
// X = Milestone version: stable milestones, planned upgrades, major application evolution.
// Y = Major version (phase/sprint): bump after successful testing when stable with no known bugs for the phase/sprint.
// Z = Patch: unit tests, minor bugfixes, design/layout tweaks, small iterations.
// Baseline: v1.0.0. Examples: v1.0.1 (Z), v1.1.0 (Y), v2.0.0 (X).
// Always tag releases to match VERSION (e.g. tag v1.0.0 when VERSION is v1.0.0).

// Repository layout (awesome-cursorrules–inspired)
// - .cursor/rules/     Project rules (.mdc); versioning.mdc applies globally.
// - rules-new/          Optional stack rules from Awesome Cursor Rules (MDC format).
// - rules/              Optional: add technology-specific .cursorrules packs per awesome-cursorrules conventions.

// General guidance
// Use clear, project-appropriate naming; keep AI-facing rules concise and actionable.
// Prefer extending existing patterns over one-off special cases.
